<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>Versio.Plc.Codesys.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <!-- EVERY TEST THAT LAUNCHES CODESYS - and, said honestly, some that do not.

         This used to be a [Trait("Toolchain", "Codesys")] tag inside Versio.Integration.Tests,
         policed by FastLaneTests, because the split it expresses is real and expensive: measured
         2026-08-17, the tests that stayed behind run in 19 seconds and the ones in here take
         half an hour. A cold CODESYS start is 17-31 s, every launch pays it in full, an import
         test needs three to five of them, and they cannot run in parallel.

         A tag was the wrong shape for that. It made "run the fast ones" a filter expression
         somebody has to remember, it put the slow suite's dependencies on the fast suite's
         project, and it needed a test whose whole job was to check the tag had been applied.
         A project boundary needs none of those: `dotnet test tests\Versio.Integration.Tests`
         is the loop, and this is the gate.

         The first line of this comment said "and nothing else" for a day, and it was false
         the day it was written: the CPython parity tests hid in here until they became
         Versio.Plc.Codesys.Parity.Tests on 2026-08-17, and the pure vendor tests - the
         planner rules, the normalizer, the scrubber, the protocol contract - hid here too,
         running in milliseconds with no toolchain, until they became
         Versio.Plc.Codesys.Pure.Tests the same day. They cost this suite nothing in time;
         what they cost was VISIBILITY, a red planner rule surfacing after half an hour of
         real CODESYS instead of after half a second. Three vendor test projects now, split
         by need: nothing, python, the real toolchain - and the first line above is finally
         just true. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Xunit.SkippableFact" Version="1.5.23" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Versio.Core\Versio.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Versio.Infrastructure\Versio.Infrastructure.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Versio.Plc.Codesys\Versio.Plc.Codesys.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Versio.Plc.Codesys.Infrastructure\Versio.Plc.Codesys.Infrastructure.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Versio.TestSupport\Versio.TestSupport.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The versio_types.py / versio_text.py None-includes that used to sit here followed
       TypeTableParityTests into Versio.Plc.Codesys.Pure.Tests on 2026-08-17 - the same
       move that stranded them once before, done right this time: the items went WITH the
       test that reads them. "A git mv moves a test; it does not move the data the test
       needs" is the finding, and its csproj carries the items' own history. -->

</Project>
